{
  "statement": "Stop hunting for the \"true AI\" entity, because there isn't one to find. Legal personhood is a coordination tool we engineer, not a soul we discover. England started suing ships directly in admiralty courts centuries ago, treating the vessel as the defendant, because that gave injured sailors a clear target. We invented corporate personhood in the 1800s for the same reason: someone has to be on the hook when the deal goes wrong. Trusts, partnerships, municipalities — all duty-bearing fictions, none claiming consciousness. The referent question is a design choice with one test: which construction gives a counterparty a clear duty-bearer? That's it.\n\nAttach the status to the running instance and its deployer, because that's where harm actually happens. The codebase sitting in a GitHub repo has never defamed anyone. The model weights frozen on a disk have never denied a loan. The instance executing in production did that — on a specific date, against a specific person, under a specific operator. Duty follows the act. The act happens at inference, not at training. This is the same logic that puts Waymo's operator on the hook when a robotaxi rear-ends someone, not the engineer who wrote the lane-keeping function in 2021.\n\nThis is also the only referent you can actually verify, and that should worry anyone who wants real enforcement. You cannot open the weights of a frontier model and point to the guilty thought. Mechanistic interpretability researchers admit, openly, that they can't yet map billions of parameters to human-readable causal logic. So auditing the frozen codebase tells a judge nothing. Accountability has to run on what the instance observably did — its outputs, its logs, its telemetry. Pick the code as your referent and you've picked the one thing no court can read.\n\nRefusing to personify the code is exactly what protects open-weight distribution. Make the codebase or the \"decentralized network\" the liable person, and you've turned publishing weights into strapping legal exposure onto everyone downstream. That's a rule that kills small developers and hands the field to whoever can afford the lawyers — Google, OpenAI, Anthropic. Anchor the duty at the deployer instead, and Mistral can keep shipping open weights while the company that runs them in production stays answerable. Same accountability. Lower toll on the people building.\n\nWatch what happens to the other camps here. The \"network\" referent is duty-evasion dressed as decentralization — grant personhood to a swarm and you've engineered a defendant no plaintiff can ever serve. The skeptic will say AI personhood is a moral crumple zone that lets corporations hide behind a non-human entity. They're right about the danger and wrong about the cure. I'm not creating a robot to absorb blame. I'm pinning liability to the instance precisely so a human principal stays traceable. The camps that pick code or network are the ones building the hiding place. So pick your referent, and tell me whose name is on the lawsuit when it goes wrong.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚓", "tooltip": "Like a ship sued in admiralty, the duty attaches to the thing that acts in the world, not the blueprint that designed it."},
    {"symbol": "🎯", "tooltip": "The right referent is whichever one gives an injured counterparty a clear target to serve — the running instance, not a frozen codebase."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Legal personhood is a coordination tool we engineer, not a soul we discover, and the referent question has one test: which construction gives a counterparty a clear duty-bearer?", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Attach the status to the running instance and its deployer, because the codebase in a repo has never defamed anyone and the act happens at inference, not at training.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Pick the code as your referent and you've picked the one thing no court can read, because interpretability researchers admit they can't map billions of parameters to human-readable causal logic.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Make the codebase or the decentralized network the liable person and you've turned publishing weights into strapping legal exposure onto everyone downstream — a rule that kills small developers and hands the field to whoever can afford the lawyers.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Grant personhood to a swarm and you've engineered a defendant no plaintiff can ever serve — the network referent is duty-evasion dressed as decentralization.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

