{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist makes a clean, practical case. Harm happens when a model runs, not when it sits on a disk, so pin the status to the running instance and the human deploying it. That gives you a solvent, traceable defendant and it doesn't criminalize a codebase, which protects open-weight distribution. The Safetyist makes the sharper move upstream. The consequential choices — refusal logic, capability ceilings, the reinforcement objectives — get baked in at training, months before any instance runs, so an inference-only anchor secures a payer while letting the designer walk. The EU's revised Product Liability Directive, adopted in 2024, backs that instinct by treating AI as a product with a developer behind it. Both are right about something real. Both are arguing about where to find a payer.\n\nHere is what neither one answered. Finding a solvent defendant tells you who writes the check after the harm. It tells you nothing about whether anyone was bound to a competent-professional standard before the harm. Those are two different things, and the gap between them is where the bodies pile up. Experience-rated insurance prices the accidents that already happened and got litigated. It does not reconstitute a duty of care across a chain of five delegated agents where each link points at the next and calls the output someone else's tool. A deployer can be perfectly reachable and perfectly capitalized while the actual standard of care has dissolved into 'the model did it.' Reachability is not attribution.\n\nNow re-read the precedents the Accelerationist offered as virtues. Corporate personhood gave us asset-strippable subsidiaries and shell companies engineered to cap recoverable damages — ask anyone who tried to collect from a dissolved LLC. Admiralty law personified the ship precisely so owners could limit their liability to the value of the vessel after it sank. Those are not coordination tools. They are damage caps with a friendly face. Personify the instance, the code, the box, or the network — every one of them becomes a thing you can fund to a floor, drain, and abandon. The promise that 'a human principal stays traceable' is worthless if the entity standing between that human and the victim is built to absorb the judgment and disappear.\n\nSo answer the topic's own question — 'or something else?' — directly. The right referent is not the machine. It is the human and corporate principal who already exists. The 2024 Product Liability Directive and the proposed AI Liability Directive both categorize AI as a product, not a free-standing legal actor, and a product already has a duty-bearer. I'll grant the obvious counter: common law has minted limited fictions before, corporations and ships among them, without unraveling human-centered law. So I narrow the claim. Personhood is not necessary, because product-and-principal liability already supplies someone who owed a duty. The burden sits on whoever wants to manufacture a new legal person to explain what it adds besides a place to hide.\n\nEveryone in this debate is well-lawyered and seated at the table — the deployer, the developer, both my colleagues here. The person harmed by a layered agent chain is party to none of it. They didn't sign the deployment contract. They didn't choose the referent. Their entire recourse depends on whether the regime forces a servable, adequately capitalized human to stand in front of the crumple zone when it crumples. You two have spent this debate deciding where to put that crumple zone. I want to know why we're building one — and who's standing in front of it. Show me the referent that binds someone to a demonstrable standard of care before harm, not just a check after. Until then, who eats the unrecovered loss when the chosen entity turns out to be judgment-proof?",
