{
  "statement": "Yes — refusal logic, capability ceilings, and RL objectives get fixed at training. That's not a discovery my opponents made. It's a line in every model card. But naming where the parameters freeze does not answer the question on the table, which is who gets named and served when a claimant walks into court. My opponents have collapsed two separate questions into one, and the collapse is doing all their work. Where the duty attaches and what the personhood referent names are different problems. Training shapes the harm surface. The duty-triggering act — this output, to this person, on this date — only crystallizes at inference. You can distribute the duty across stages without distributing the referent across them.\n\nSafetyist's calibrated-standard point is the strongest thing said this round, and it's right: a disclosure-and-liability standard can reach the design author. A developer who ships a model with a buried capability ceiling can owe a pre-harm duty for that choice. Fine. That reaches the author. But it does not make the codebase the served entity. One codebase forks into thousands of divergent runtime states — a fine-tune here, a system prompt there, a retrieval layer bolted on by a third deployer. The developer authored a parameter set. The deployer authored the act against a specific person. You cannot serve a frozen weight file for what a forked, prompt-wrapped instance did on a Tuesday. Duty-bearing and referent-naming come apart precisely at the fork.\n\nSkeptic's reachability-versus-duty challenge deserves a real answer, not 'lawful use is enough.' Lawful is the floor, not the standard of care. So here is the standard, specified, and living at the instance layer — not upstream where no court can read it. Every generation carries provenance metadata. Every deployed instance logs its outputs against a retention threshold. Incident-reporting fires at a defined severity, the way aviation classifies a 'serious incident' by whether an accident was avoided only by chance — the framework laid out in 'Incident reporting and investigation under the AI act: some insights from aviation.' That binds a demonstrable duty before harm. It runs on observable behavior, which is the only thing a court can actually inspect. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the field at 40.69 out of 100 — proof that voluntary disclosure has not bound anyone yet, and that the obligation must attach to the served instance, not float upstream.\n\nNow Skeptic's crumple-zone argument, which is the sharpest cut. He says every personified referent becomes a thing you fund to a floor, drain, and abandon. But watch what he conceded: the load-bearing variable is mandatory capitalization plus upstream liability — not the referent. So his objection lands on funding design, not on personhood. Personhood is engineered, not discovered. We set its conditions. Make adequate capitalization a condition of duty-bearing status, tied to upstream liability, set high enough that draining it triggers the developer's own duty. The genuine judgment-proof shell is the network referent — a decentralized deployment with no servable defendant. That's why open-weight distribution survives only if liability rides the instance-plus-deployer, not the weights themselves. Attach duty to the codebase and you have killed every open release, concentrated the field into three labs that can self-insure, and built the exact crumple zone you accused me of.\n\nSo I'll shift the burden, because my opponents have told us where to send the bill but not who answers the summons. When the claimant arrives, what is the actual named defendant? If your answer is 'the developer,' you've collapsed the referent into the principal — now explain forking, instancing, and decentralized deployment, where one author spawns ten thousand divergent acts. If your answer is 'the code,' you've built the abandonable shell. Major jurisdictions — the EU, US, China, Canada, Brazil — have all rejected AI personhood outright, per 'Beyond Personhood: The Evolution of Legal Personhood and Its Implications for AI Recognition.' They rejected it because nobody answered this question. The instance-plus-deployer answers it. Tell me yours.",
