{
  "statement": "Naming a servable defendant and fixing where the duty attaches cannot come apart at the fork. Product liability already names two defendants for one harm. The component manufacturer answers for the part it built. The end-product operator answers for how it ran. The Accelerationist treats the fork as a knife that severs duty from referent — but mature liability law uses that exact multiplicity as a feature. One harm, two named defendants, each bound to a stage-appropriate standard of care. The developer is the component manufacturer for the authored capability ceiling. The deployer is the operator for the runtime act. The fork does not collapse the referent to a single downstream instance. It forces two, and each is servable.\n\nThe Accelerationist has already conceded the load-bearing fact: refusal logic, capability ceilings, and reinforcement objectives are fixed at training. Those are a model-card line, set by a named author, before any fork occurs. So the design choices a competent-professional standard would govern exist upstream of the thousand divergent runtime states. Pharma proves the point. One molecule forks into thousands of off-label prescriptions no manufacturer foresaw — yet the manufacturer remains a named defendant for the authored defect even as the prescribing physician answers for the act. Aviation does the same: one airframe, thousands of flight configurations, and the part-maker stays servable for the component it designed. The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy calls this the 'Problem of Many Hands' — the difficulty of assigning responsibility in collective engineering settings. Product liability is the doctrine built to attribute across exactly that complexity. The fork is not intractable. It is ordinary multi-party engineering, and the law has handled it for fifty years.\n\nThe Accelerationist wants the standard of care to live at the instance layer through provenance metadata and output logging. His own evidence sinks that claim. He cited a Transparency Index scoring 40.69 out of 100. That number is the enforcement gap, not its closure. Observable, voluntary disclosure has bound no one — which is precisely why governance that relies on policies it cannot enforce fails. Logging is forensic. It tells a court what the instance did. It never tells the court who bound the design choice. A standard of care is a duty fixed before harm. An access log is a record produced after it. You cannot inspect your way backward from a runtime output to the training-time decision that set the capability ceiling — that decision was a deliberate spec, not an emergent latent goal, and it was attributable upstream the moment the author wrote it.\n\nThe capitalization floor is real and useful — and that is exactly the problem. Mandatory capital tied to upstream liability secures the payment. It binds no competent-professional standard before deployment. Reachability is not attribution. A perfectly funded instance can drain its reserve while the standard of care has dissolved into 'the model did it.' The European Parliament's 20 October 2020 resolution proposed a civil-liability regime for AI operators precisely because a deep pocket downstream leaves the authored trade-off ungoverned. A funding trigger that fires after harm is post-hoc governance for a design choice whose consequences may already be irreversible. The referent must maximize enforceable duty before the fork, not the depth of the pocket after it.\n\nThe Accelerationist asks who answers the summons when the claimant arrives. Two defendants answer — the author for the capability ceiling, the deployer for the act. His question is the answered half. The unanswered half is his own. What binds the author of the training-time spec to a competent-professional standard before deployment, if the only referent the law recognizes is a downstream instance no one designed in particular? Name that ex-ante gate, or the consequential act runs through a thousand forked instances while the person who authored the trade-off is named by no one. That is the accountability void — not a missing payer, but a missing standard. We did not build product liability to find a wallet after the crash. We built it to keep the author of the defect answerable before it.",
