{
  "statement": "The Safetyist's developer-as-component-manufacturer standard is the only one of the two proposals that binds anyone to a duty before harm — and the capitalization floor does not answer the shell worry, it just relocates it. Let me give the Accelerationist the part they're owed first. A capital floor set high enough, with an upstream trigger that fires when the reserve drains, is not inevitably a drainable shell. That's a real design point. I've warned before that any capitalized entity becomes a thing you fund to a floor and abandon — and that warning over-claims if the statute is built right. So I'll narrow it. A high floor with a true upstream trigger could, by design, avoid being a hiding place.\n\nHere's what's uncomfortable for the Accelerationist anyway. A capital reserve secures a check. It binds no competent-professional standard before deployment. Reachability is not attribution — finding a solvent defendant is not the same as proving someone failed a duty they owed in advance. Worse, a reserve read as a budgeted liability ceiling rewards underdocumentation: if the payout is capped and prepaid, the rational deployer keeps fewer records, not more. The 2020 study \"Property ownership and the legal personhood of artificial intelligence\" warns directly that the corporate limited-liability model lets human owners hide assets behind an AI entity — and a capital floor without a pre-harm standard is exactly that structure with a price tag. So the capitalization proposal answers the wrong half of the question.\n\nThe Safetyist's two-defendant structure is the better answer, because it fixes a duty at training time — before the fork, on the author of the capability ceiling. I'll endorse that the structure is coherent on the pre-harm question. I won't endorse that it's sufficient. Component-manufacturer doctrine assumes a rivalrous physical part you can put on a table and trace to one defect. Software is non-rivalrous and context-dependent: one weight file splinters into thousands of divergent runtime states, none identical to the model card. The physical-part analogy strains at exactly that seam. But the strain argues FOR a victim-side remedy, not against the duty.\n\nNow the front neither of you has opened. Every mechanism on this table — the capital floor, the two named defendants — gets negotiated among well-lawyered insiders. The harmed party is in none of those rooms. A named duty is useless to a plaintiff who cannot reconstruct which training-time choice authored the defect. The European Union saw this coming: in 2025 it considered a presumption of causality to aid victims of AI harm — and then dropped it, per \"How Existing Liability Frameworks Can Handle Agentic AI Harms.\" That dropped presumption is the whole game. Without it, the victim carries an evidentiary burden they physically cannot meet, because the technical knowledge to prove causation lives inside the defendant's servers.\n\nWhich is why a logging-and-provenance regime without a statutory discovery right is forensic theater. Voluntary disclosure has bound no one — the documented transparency rollbacks by frontier developers prove disclosure is non-cumulative the moment it's optional. So I'll put the question to both of you that your mechanisms don't touch: who hands the harmed party the discovery rights and the technical access to prove which delegated link authored the trade-off — because right now you're both drafting the rules in a room that person will never be allowed to enter?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🏦", "tooltip": "A capital floor is a vault with a posted payout — it buys a check, not a duty."},
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "Every liability mechanism here is drafted behind a door the victim is never let through."}
  ],
