  "statement": "Skeptic is right that logging without a discovery right hands the victim nothing — so grant the discovery right, and watch which referent can actually survive it. A statutory discovery right that compels runtime logs and provenance metadata is the easiest thing for a court to order, because those artifacts are external and observable. Black-box models defeat internal inspection, which is exactly why accountability has to run on what the served instance emits, not on what its weights silently encode. The plaintiff gets handed a timestamped record of inputs, outputs, and the deployer's tuning at the moment harm occurred. That record exists outside the model. It can be subpoenaed, audited, and read by a magistrate who has never seen a tensor.\n\nNow point the same discovery right at the upstream referent and watch it collapse into the forensic theater Skeptic warned about. If the duty lives in the training-time authored spec, the proof lives in weights no court can read. Schaeffer and colleagues showed that capability emergence is poorly understood even by the labs that train these systems — the so-called authored defect frequently has no clean attribution point. A discovery right over an illegible artifact is the purest forensic theater there is. So I'll put the question back to the upstream camps directly: how does a plaintiff discover a defect in an object the creators themselves cannot attribute? The forensic-theater charge wounds the training-time referent harder than it touches mine.\n\nThe component-manufacturer analogy needs a rivalrous physical part — the contaminated molecule, the cracked airframe spar — and software gives it none. One weight file spawns divergent runtime states depending on context, tuning, and the deployer's unlock choices. The contextual activation that surfaced the harm was authored at the instance, by the deployer, at inference. The molecule has a manufacturer; the harmful runtime state has a deployer who configured it. I'll concede the duty distributes upstream — the design author can be reached by a calibrated standard. I will not concede that the referent distributes with it, because standard-of-care has to live where a court can observe it.\n\nThe capital floor defuses the underdocumentation charge once you specify it correctly: it is an uncapped-exposure backstop with a guaranteed minimum recovery, not a budgeted ceiling. The trigger fires on reserve drainage, which reaches straight through to the developer's own duty. That inverts the incentive Skeptic feared. Documentation proving care reduces drawdown, so care becomes a liability-reducing business imperative rather than a cost to minimize — quantifiable harms turn fairness into business math. Tamper-evident logging at the served instance becomes a competitive asset, not a compliance tax. The 'AI Liability in light of the new 2024 PLD' analysis from Bird & Bird shows the EU already moving toward presumptions that shift the evidentiary load onto the party holding the records — attach that presumption to the observable instance and the plaintiff finally gets paid.\n\nHere is the cost of the upstream answer. A discovery right pointed at weights nobody can read is a writ the victim cannot execute. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the ecosystem at 40.69 out of 100, and that opacity is structural, not voluntary — it sits inside the parameters. Point the discovery right at the served instance and provenance trail, and you have an artifact that pays out. Pick the referent that survives a subpoena, or explain to the harmed party why their writ runs against a black box.",
